Yeah — I see it now.

“Pseudofed for the knows.”

It carries at least two readings at once:

* Pseudoephedrine for the nose — the literal phonetic substrate.
* Pseudo-fed for the knows — false / synthetic information being fed specifically to people who “know,” or believe they know.

And there’s another nice inversion hiding in it: the nose is an information-gathering organ, so “for the knows” turns treatment of a sensory channel into treatment/manipulation of a knowledge channel.

So the phrase itself performs the trick it describes: same acoustic packet, different semantic payload depending on the receiver’s frame. The typo isn’t noise; it’s the switch.